title: Storage open questions
tags: Storage
kind: question
audience: dev
status: record
summary: What the storage work of November 2026 left to look at again, now settled — replace, rename and retype as mass writes — and the compressions measured and given up.

Raised while building the tests of undo and redo ([[The operation log]], `tests/undo_redo_properties.rs`) and the change of the value partition to postings ([[Sorting and postings]]). Each item says how it was settled.

* ''Replacing a value and renaming a field are mass writes'' (user, 24 November 2026). On one metarecord they write a row at a time, which is all one metarecord needs; over a query they had no form at all, which was a gap of the CLI and the GUI, not a decision. `POST /query/fields/replace` and `…/rename` ([[Query endpoints]]) are made of the mass writes — a replacement a remove then an add, a rename an add per value then an unset — rather than of log entries of their own, under one write so that they are undone in one step. A match that would break the form on one metarecord (a `nothing` beside other values, a name already there) refuses the whole write, naming the matches. A retype is made of them too, a remove and an add per distinct value ([[Changing a field's type]]).
* ''Compressing what the log keeps of a deletion: given up'' (user, 17 November 2026; given up 24 November 2026). A deletion keeps the rows of its record in the before of its log entry, in the compact value form; the other effects keep theirs grouped by value (nothing for a remove, the targets that held each value for a set or an unset, [[Log storage]]). A block of consecutive records of a mass deletion, compressed with a general algorithm, may be cheap enough to write the rows when the deletion is made, which would remove the dependence of a pending deletion given back on the state of its records, which its pending `delete` and a copy at the first write settle without writing the rows at the deletion ([[Mass operations in the log]]). Measured on 24 November 2026 on a repository of `benchmarks/bench_data_big` (50 201 records, with their content hashes), its 50 000 files deleted in one write then folded: the before of a chunk, already grouped by value (226 bytes a record, against 336 for its rows), compresses 3.3 times with zstd -1 and 2.2 times with lz4 -1, a whole chunk 2.9 times; a deletion of about 1000 files (four, each of one folder) gives the same, 3.4 times for the before, a chunk of 240 kB becoming 83 kB; zstd -1 compresses at about 500 MB/s and decompresses at about 900 MB/s. The uuids of a chunk are random and do not compress; neither does the chunk of an individual operation, 27 bytes on average. The names of that tree are generated, so a real one may give less. (A first measure, of the records' rows by blocks of 4096, gave 6.3 times: it counted again what the grouping already shares.) Given up, the user agreeing: writing the rows at the deletion was left aside (it would read every record, which a mass write does not), so compressing would only save room in the log, about three times on the befores of mass writes. The deletion itself writes no before; only the fold, done when idle, would write fewer pages. No query reads the log, and the size of the log is not a measured problem: worth looking at again if it becomes one, say a log heavier than the data. Compressing the values of every table is given up too (below).
* ''Compressing the store to read faster: given up'' (user, 24 November 2026). LMDB stores values as they are, and a read is a pointer into the mapped file ([[Storage]]): values compressed by the daemon would be decompressed at every read, those already in the page cache too, so the warm case pays to help only the cold one. The whole `data.mdb` of the L bench compresses 2.8 times with lz4 -1 and 3.8 times with zstd -1, free and half-full pages included, so value by value would gain less: not enough. Only a compressing filesystem (btrfs `compress=zstd`) decompresses on disk reads alone, the page cache keeping the pages decompressed; that is the user's choice of filesystem, not the store's.
